hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-65 : List (List Word)
hyperactivity-is-the-theory-of-knowledge-graphs-lines-65 = cons "Is this object a graph or a hypergraph? Neither word is wide enough, and both appear as truncations of what type theory already forces. A binary graph restricts every edge to two untyped endpoints joined by an inert label. In hyperactivity, a relation is a dependent type family R : (x₁ : A₁) → ... → (xₙ : Aₙ) → Type whose later endpoints can depend on earlier ones; an inhabitant p : R a₁ ... aₙ is a witnessed dependent n-ary hyperedge of arbitrary arity (n = 0 for a standalone proposition, n = 1 for a predicate, n = 2 for a binary relation, n ≥ 3 for a reaction, derivation, or multi-party warrant) with no reification hacks or blank-node encodings. Moreover, identity is not a flat equivalence relation: paths connect 0-cells, dependent paths (`PathP`, Part I §4.4) and cubical path constructors (such as `Bag`'s `comm`, `assoc`, and `unitl`, composed by `comp` in §3.4) connect 1-cells, and higher cubes connect k-cells, turning the store into a higher-dimensional dependent hypergraph (a cubical type / ∞-groupoid of constructions). Classical RDF triples and property graphs are the 1-dimensional, 2-ary, proof-irrelevant truncation of this object; hypergraphs recover arbitrary arity while still truncating dependency and higher cells." (cons "" nil)
